cdi wrote:
> The high priests on the astral plane are supposed to be anonymized.

Supposed to be anonymous via telepathy or any other long-range effect
that could alert the player which altar to move towards upon entering
the level, you mean?

> #chatting with the high priests gives a message beginning with "The
> high priest of so-and-so"

If you can physically see the priest, ie. he's in your visible
line-of-sight, then this should be fine.  As Janis says, however, if you
can get #chat to work from a distance (say, through walls or through two
closed doors), this would be a problem.

> #chatting with no visible cash reveals whether the high priest is
> co-aligned or not.  The high priest is either "not interested" or
> "gives you two bits for an ale".

See above.  If the priest is in line-of-sight, you're OK.

> In both cases, its possible to identify the correctness of the priest's
> associated altar without standing on it while unblind.

There are many other cases in which it is possible to do this; ie. "The
high priest of Bob drinks a potion of gain level!".  Etc.  All of those
depend on line-of-sight, so there is plenty of consistency here.

> I'd like some second opinions on whether or not these constitute bugs.
> The first one, I think is definately a bug.  The second one, I'm not so
> sure about.

I believe neither of these are bugs.  I think that instead, your assumed
premise is faulty; specifically, that the player should only be allowed
to determine the alignment of a particular altar by standing on it.

